course: religion-ethics
lesson: 65
title: How other people remember: Día de los Muertos, Qingming and more
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: All around the world people say goodbye to the dead and remember them, and the customs look very different: bright orange flowers and sugar skulls in Mexico, swept graves in China, a week at home for a Jewish family, three days of songs on a Māori marae, a funeral with no prayers for a humanist.
explain: Picture Ava's class looking at a Mexican ofrenda, a home altar for Día de los Muertos: photos of Grandma, candles, sweet bread, marigolds and her favourite chocolate; it is not a creepy party but a joyful «we remember you and what you loved».
explain: Each custom comes with a hope or belief: Hindus believe the soul is reborn in a new body, Christians hope for resurrection, Muslims believe the soul goes to God and awaits the Day of Judgement, many Chinese families honour ancestors whatever their religion, and humanists believe this life is the one we know and should be celebrated.
explain: We can learn about a friend's customs with respect, ask what helps, and never laugh at them; our own family may do things differently, and that is fine.
explain: Asked what all these customs have in common, Ava's class finds the answer: almost every people remembers its dead, gathers together, often shares food, and shows love and respect in its own way.
fact: Día de los Muertos (Day of the Dead) is celebrated in Mexico on 1 and 2 November to remember and celebrate loved ones who have died.
fact: It mixes Catholic All Saints' and All Souls' Days with older Indigenous Mexican traditions; families decorate graves with orange marigolds and make sugar skulls.
fact: An ofrenda is an altar with photos, candles, food and favourite things of the dead, offered as a sign of love and welcome; most families do not think the dead really eat it, though some believe the spirits enjoy its smell.
fact: Qingming, or Tomb-Sweeping Day, falls in early April; Chinese families clean their ancestors' graves and bring food and flowers.
fact: Some Chinese families burn special paper money and paper houses, a sign of caring for ancestors' needs in the next life; it is not real money.
fact: At a Hindu funeral the body is usually cremated; traditionally the eldest son leads the rites, though today daughters sometimes do too.
fact: Hindus believe in reincarnation: the soul is reborn in a new body until it is finally freed.
fact: Jewish families «sit shiva», a week of mourning at home, when friends visit with food and comfort; mourners say the Kaddish, a prayer praising God.
fact: Muslims usually bury their dead within a day if possible, as a sign of respect; the body is washed, wrapped in white cloth and laid facing Mecca.
fact: A tangihanga, or tangi, is a Māori funeral, often held on a marae (a Māori meeting place) over about three days; the body is not left alone, and people sing and give speeches.
fact: A humanist funeral has no prayers; a celebrant tells the story of the person's life and plays music they loved, and it is a real funeral.
fact: Almost every culture remembers its dead, gathers people together, often shares food, and shows love and respect in its own way.
exercise: Ava learns about Día de los Muertos. What is it for? | Remembering and celebrating loved ones who have died | Scaring people with skeleton costumes and loud fireworks in the streets | Celebrating the end of the harvest | Marking the start of the school year
exercise: When is Día de los Muertos? | 1 and 2 November | 25 and 26 December | 1 and 2 January each year | The last day of March
exercise: What does an ofrenda hold? | Photos, candles, food and the person's favourite things | Only money given to the church by the family, to pay the priest | Presents wrapped up for the children of the house to open | The person's old school reports, certificates and exam papers
exercise: Do families think the dead eat the chocolate on the ofrenda? | Mostly not; it is a sign of love, though some believe spirits enjoy the smell | Yes, it always vanishes by morning | Yes, the dead must be fed or they get angry | No, the chocolate is just there for decoration
exercise: Mei's family sweeps graves at Qingming. What happens then? | Families clean ancestors' graves and bring food and flowers | Families sweep the whole city's streets for the New Year | Families move old graves to a new cemetery outside the town | Families build new houses for the living
exercise: Mei's grandma burns paper money. Is it real money? | No, it is special paper, a sign of caring for ancestors | Yes, real banknotes taken out of the bank | Yes, but only coins, never paper notes | No, it is old homework that she never liked and wants gone
exercise: At Priya's Hindu funeral, her uncle, the eldest son, leads the prayers. Why? | Tradition gives the eldest son this role, though daughters sometimes lead now | The eldest son always pays for the funeral | Hindu law says only uncles can pray | He is the only one who knows the prayers
exercise: What do Hindus believe happens to the soul? | It is reborn in a new body until it is freed | It sleeps quietly in the grave until the spring comes | It becomes a bright new star high up in the night sky | It disappears completely and at once, the moment the body dies
exercise: Ben's Jewish family «sit shiva». What is shiva? | A week of mourning at home, with visitors bringing food | A Jewish winter festival with fireworks, dancing and presents for the children | A special chair kept only for the grandfather of the family | A long silent walk to the cemetery every single morning of the year
exercise: What prayer do Jewish mourners say? | The Kaddish | The Lord's Prayer | The Hail Mary | The Gayatri Mantra
exercise: Zara's Muslim neighbour is buried the next day. Why so quick? | Muslims bury within a day if possible, as a sign of respect | The family was in a hurry to go on holiday | The cemetery was closing for the winter and would not open again until spring | It was a mistake by the hospital
exercise: Aroha's family stays with her grandfather's body on the marae for three days. What is a tangihanga? | A Māori funeral where the body is not left alone | A Māori wedding feast | A kind of Māori dance competition held every summer on the beach | A Māori sports day
exercise: Jack's humanist uncle's funeral has no prayers. Is it a real funeral? | Yes, it celebrates his life and comforts those left behind | No, a funeral needs a priest or a vicar there for it to count as a real one | No, it is only a party with music | Yes, but only if a hymn is sung
exercise: What do all these customs have in common? | Remembering the dead, gathering together and showing love | They all use exactly the same prayers in the same language | They all burn paper money | They all happen on the same day
exercise: Where does Día de los Muertos come from? | Catholic All Souls' Day mixed with older Indigenous Mexican traditions | A Hollywood film made in 1990 | An American shop's Halloween sale | A Spanish football club's celebration
exercise: Which way is a Muslim body laid in the grave? | Facing Mecca | Facing the sunrise | Facing the family's home | Facing the sea
test: A kid says, «Day of the Dead is just Mexican Halloween, it's lowkey spooky.» («Lowkey» means «a bit».) What is more accurate? | It's a joyful festival to remember loved ones, not a scary night | Yes, it's exactly the same as Halloween, with trick-or-treating and pumpkins | It's a festival to scare away the dead | It's a day when nobody may speak
test: Lily, puzzled, says, «They clean graves? That's like housework for dead people.» What is the respectful reply? | Caring for graves shows respect for the family's ancestors | It's a punishment for naughty children | They clean them so the old graves can be sold to new families who need one | It's done to stop ghosts escaping
test: A London lad says, «Burning money? That's bare dumb, bruv.» («Bare» means «really»; «bruv» means «mate».) What is fair? | It's special paper, and a custom showing care for ancestors | He's right, it is real cash thrown away for nothing, just to show off | It's to get rid of fake money | It's a magic trick for tourists
test: What would happen if everyone thought their own funeral customs were the only right ones? | People might mock each other's grief instead of respecting it | Everyone would agree on everything | All funerals would become the same | Nobody would ever be sad again
test: Sam asks Ben, «What's shiva, is it like a sleepover?» Ben explains. What is true? | A week of mourning at home where friends visit with food | A party with games and music every night of the week | A sleepover at the synagogue for children | A week of fasting at home with no visitors allowed in at all
test: Zara's Gen Z sister says, «Buried the next day? That's so rushed, it's giving chaos.» («It's giving» means «it looks like».) What is the true answer? | Quick burial is a Muslim custom of respect, not chaos | It is chaos; they simply forgot to plan the funeral properly in time | It's to save money on the funeral | It only happens in very hot countries
test: Aroha, a Kiwi girl, says, «Chur for coming to Koro's tangi, we'll be on the marae three days.» («Chur» means «thanks»; «Koro» means «Grandad».) What is she inviting people to? | Her grandfather's Māori funeral, with singing and speeches | Her grandad's birthday party at a beach | A three-day camping trip with her class, the teachers and some parents | A rugby match at the school
test: Jack's cousin asks, «Is it still a real funeral if there's no God stuff?» What is the best answer? | Yes, a humanist funeral is a real way to say goodbye | No, without any prayers or hymns it doesn't really count as a funeral | No, it's just a concert with sad songs | Only if the vicar comes along to bless it
test: Priya's mate from Delhi says, «My nani's funeral, the eldest son lit the fire, it is what it is, na?» («Nani» means «Granny».) What is true about the eldest son? | Tradition gives him this role; today daughters sometimes do it too | Only sons may ever attend a Hindu funeral | The eldest son must pay for everything | He lights the fire to keep the family warm
test: An ethics judge asks: «Why might all these different customs share food?» What is the best-reasoned answer? | Eating together comforts people and keeps them close in grief | Because the dead are hungry and must be fed by the family every single day | Because funerals are really birthday parties | Because food is cheaper than flowers
test: A cartoon shows the Day of the Dead as skeletons chasing children through the streets. What is wrong? | It is a loving festival of memory, not a scary chase | Nothing, it is exactly like that every year in Mexico | Skeletons only chase adults | The skeletons are real people
test: Ava's teacher asks the class, formally, «What, if anything, do these customs share?» What is the best answer? | Remembering the dead, coming together and showing love in their own way | They all use the same holy book | They are all Christian, but held in different countries around the whole world | They all forbid any food
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the festival of Día de los Muertos? | A celebration in memory of deceased loved ones, combining Catholic and Indigenous traditions | A festival for the frightening of children by masked persons | An occasion for the commemoration of military victories | A festival of the harvest without reference to the dead
exam: Identify the principal activity undertaken by Chinese families during the Qingming festival. | The cleaning of ancestral graves and the offering of food and flowers | The construction of new dwellings for the living | The relocation of graves to new cemeteries beyond the boundaries of the city | The burning of genuine currency for amusement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Jewish observance of shiva. | A week of mourning observed at home, during which visitors bring food and comfort | A festival of lights observed in winter | The recitation of prayers solely by the rabbi | The immediate resumption of ordinary affairs following burial
exam: Ascertain the feature common to the customs of mourning described in this lesson. | The remembrance of the dead and the gathering of the living in love and respect | The use of an identical form of words in every tradition | The cremation of the deceased in every tradition | The prohibition of food and music in every tradition
